// ─── boundary/env-access ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Tag:      boundary
// Mechanism: GritQL (per-file, real-time)
// Blocking: Yes
//
// Prevents: Any module other than the env module reading process.env
//           (or import.meta.env) directly.
//
// Applies:  All src/** files EXCEPT the env module itself and tests.
//
// ── Why this is a boundary rule and not a style preference ────────────
//
// The env module does not just read variables — it decides what their
// ABSENCE MEANS, and those decisions differ by bundle. A missing
// analytics key might be "off" in development and a thrown error in a
// release build; a malformed one throws in both. A second reader gets
// the raw string and none of that reasoning, so it lands on its own
// answer, and the two answers disagree in exactly the case nobody tests.
//
// The cost of that is rarely a crash. It is a system that reports to the
// wrong place, or runs against the wrong backend, and looks fine doing
// it — a number that is quietly wrong from then on. One reader is what
// keeps the decision in one `if`.
//
// The second reason is bundling, and it applies wherever a bundler
// inlines variables by literal text match (Vite's import.meta.env, Expo's
// EXPO_PUBLIC_*, Next's NEXT_PUBLIC_*). A computed lookup such as
// process.env[name] is left alone and reads as undefined in a production
// bundle. Keeping every read in one module keeps every read in the shape
// the bundler recognises.
//
// ── Adapt ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 1. The owning module (the exclusion line). One file, named exactly.
//    Projects that split client and server env exclude both:
//      ! $filename <: r".*/src/env\.(?:client|server)\.ts$",
//
// 2. The access form. This template matches `process.env`. Add
//    `import.meta.env` as a second arm for Vite-family projects; for a
//    project using only import.meta.env, swap it in.
//
// 3. Build-time config files (vite.config.ts, app.config.ts, next.config.js)
//    sit outside src/ and are unaffected. That is deliberate: what the
//    BUILD reads is a different question from what a RUNNING app
//    believes, and conflating them forces an exemption that then gets
//    reused for runtime code.
//
// ── Why six arms ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Reading env has six spellings and they are six different CST shapes.
// Five of them are not things anyone writes by accident: `process["env"]`,
// `globalThis.process`, destructuring `env` off `process`, and importing
// `env` from `node:process` are what someone reaches for once this rule
// stops them the obvious way. A containment rule that leaves the ways
// around it open is a speed bump, and it is worse than no rule, because
// the header claims a guarantee the pattern does not deliver.
//
// Verified against Biome 2.5.5: all six fire, and a legal `myObject.env`
// stays silent.
//
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

file(name=$filename, body=$program) where {
    $filename <: r".*/src/.*",

    // --- The one module allowed to read env ---
    ! $filename <: r".*/src/env\.ts$",

    // --- Global exclusions (shared across all rules) ---
    ! $filename <: r".*\.test\.[tj]sx?$|.*\.integration\.test\.[tj]sx?$|.*__tests__.*|.*src/test/.*",
    ! $filename <: r".*/scripts/.*",

    $program <: contains bubble or {
        `process.env`,
        `process[$_]`,
        `globalThis.process`,
        `import.meta.env`,
        `const { env } = process`,
        JsModuleSource() as $m where { $m <: r"[\"']node:process[\"']" }
    } as $access where {
        register_diagnostic(
            span = $access,
            message = "Only the env module reads process.env. Import the validated config it exports instead, and add an entry there when something needs a new variable. That module does not merely read the variable — it decides what its absence MEANS, and the answer differs between a development and a production bundle. A second reader gets the raw string and none of that decision, so it invents its own answer and the two disagree in the case nobody tests, which usually surfaces as a system reporting somewhere wrong rather than as a crash. Reads here can also be invisible to the bundler: public env vars are inlined by literal text match, so anything the bundler does not recognise is undefined in production."
        )
    }
}
